# Target C-II — physical hubward pieces sharpen the support normal form

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` structural strengthening; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** any positive-density one-bar obstruction family on one side of the common high-`t` routing hub, with application to the corrected exact-antipodal/strong-TB1 fork through `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** common diameter-routing hub; one-sided interval support; convex diameter-thrackle order; two host-side layer arcs; one-turn decomposition `Q=Q^uparrow union T_m union Q^downarrow`; unconditional one-bar blocker fork  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_physical_piece_support_normal_form_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The preceding one-bar support normal form used unimodality and lost factors `1/2,1/2,1/2`, leaving a uniform density factor `1/8`. There is a simpler physical decomposition which is stronger.

After fixing one birth host side, every hubward partner endpoint lies in exactly one of three boundary pieces:

\[
Q^\uparrow,
\qquad T_m,
\qquad Q^\downarrow.
\]

Pigeonhole one piece directly. On either host piece the path-region labels are monotone, so the support-end tags are automatically monotone. On the terminal piece every tag equals `m`.

Thus the arbitrary one-death alternative can be sharpened: a nonterminal death plateau is already a fixed host-to-host rail. The only genuinely non-host physical alternative is concentration on the far terminal boundary piece.

---

## Theorem PP — physical-piece support normal form

Let `Z_D` be a family of nonhub interfaces on one fixed side of the common routing hub, with one selected actual switching diameter bar `h_i in X_i` per interface and

\[
|Z_D|>zD-o(D),
\qquad z>0.
\tag{PP.1}

Assume for notation that the interfaces are pre-hub. Then one of the following three alternatives contains more than

\[
\boxed{{z\over6}D-o(D)}
\tag{PP.2}

selected bars.

### (A) Host-to-host staircase

All birth endpoints lie on one fixed pre-hub host arc and all partner endpoints lie on one fixed hubward host arc. In interface order

\[
i_1<i_2<\cdots<i_M<k,
\qquad
b_1\le b_2\le\cdots\le b_M,
\tag{PP.3}
\]

with `b_s>=k`. Hence

\[
I_s=\{i_s+1,\ldots,b_s\}
\tag{PP.4}
\]

is a proper hub-crossing support staircase.

### (B) Host-to-host nested rail

The same fixed-host conclusion holds, but

\[
b_1\ge b_2\ge\cdots\ge b_M\ge k,
\tag{PP.5}
\]

so

\[
\boxed{
I_1\supsetneq I_2\supsetneq\cdots\supsetneq I_M.
}
\tag{PP.6}

### (C) Far-terminal nested rail

All birth endpoints lie on one fixed pre-hub host arc and all partner endpoints lie on the far terminal boundary piece `T_m`. Therefore

\[
\boxed{b_1=b_2=\cdots=b_M=m}
\tag{PP.7}

and again

\[
I_1\supsetneq I_2\supsetneq\cdots\supsetneq I_M.
\tag{PP.8}

Thus the only physical alternative to a linear fixed-host rail is a linear family whose hubward endpoints all lie on the far terminal piece and whose supports all persist to the terminal path region.

---

## 1. Fix one birth host side

Every selected pre-hub switch starts at its tagged interface. Its birth endpoint lies in the corresponding middle layer `R_i`, and the rectangle layer meets the outer boundary in two host-side subarcs `C_i^+` and `C_i^-`.

Color each selected bar by its birth side. One class has size

\[
>{z\over2}D-o(D).
\tag{PP.9}

Fix it. Birth endpoints are distinct and the subarcs `C_i^sigma` occur monotonically along one physical host arc as the interface tag changes.

Every selected support contains the common hub cut, so every partner endpoint lies on the complementary hubward shore

\[
Q=Q^\uparrow\cup T_m\cup Q^\downarrow.
\tag{PP.10}

---

## 2. Fix one hubward physical piece

Partition the fixed-birth-side family according to whether its partner endpoint lies in `Q^uparrow`, `T_m`, or `Q^downarrow`. One class has size

\[
\boxed{>{z\over6}D-o(D).}
\tag{PP.11}

Partner collisions are allowed. If two partners are distinct, the selected diameter bars are vertex-disjoint and must cross, so convex chord order makes the partner map weakly order-preserving. If partners coincide, record a tie. Thus partner boundary position is monotone in birth boundary position.

Since birth boundary position itself is monotone in interface index up to one reversal, the selected partner positions are monotone in interface index up to a reversal.

---

## 3. A host piece forces monotone support ends

On `Q^uparrow`, path-region labels are weakly increasing from `R_k` to `R_m`. On `Q^downarrow`, they are weakly decreasing from `R_m` to `R_k`.

Therefore, if the selected physical piece is either host arc, the support-end tags

\[
b_1,b_2,\ldots,b_M
\tag{PP.12}

are monotone in increasing interface order. Depending on the two harmless orientation reversals, they are either nondecreasing or nonincreasing.

The nondecreasing case is (A). The nonincreasing case is (B), because increasing starts plus nonincreasing ends imply strict support nesting.

No unimodal subsequence extraction is needed.

---

## 4. The terminal piece forces one common terminal support end

Every point of `T_m` lies in the far terminal path region `R_m`. Hence if the selected partner class lies in `T_m`, every selected support end is exactly

\[
b_s=m.
\tag{PP.13}

Increasing birth indices then give the strict nesting (PP.8).

This sharpens the earlier death-plateau language. If a constant death tag satisfies `b_*<m`, its partner endpoints cannot lie in `T_m`, whose label is `m`. They therefore lie on a hubward host arc and are already part of a host-to-host rail. Only the terminal tag `m` can support a genuinely non-host physical plateau.

The theorem does **not** assert a bounded number of source vertices on `T_m`; a linear terminal-piece family is not yet excluded.

---

## 5. Endpoint-disjoint extraction

Birth endpoints are distinct. The only collisions are among partners. Using

\[
\Delta(H)\le(4\,3^{1/8}+o(1))D^{3/4},
\tag{PP.14}
\]

greedy selection of at most one retained bar per partner vertex gives, in whichever branch realizes (PP.2),

\[
\boxed{
>
\left({z\over24\,3^{1/8}}-o(1)\right)D^{1/4}
}
\tag{PP.15}

endpoint-disjoint bars. They are therefore pairwise-crossing diameter chords.

---

## 6. Unconditional current high-`t` application

The corrected exact-antipodal/strong-TB1 fork supplies one mechanism of density

\[
z=s_0={7c_0\over16}
={791\over320{,}000{,}000},
\qquad
c_0={113\over20{,}000{,}000}.
\tag{PP.16}

Choose one actual switching bar per interface from that mechanism: one member of the exact force pair in the exact-antipodal case, or the chosen strong blocker in the strong-TB1 case.

Theorem PP gives one physical support normal form with density

\[
\boxed{
>{s_0\over6}D-o(D)
={791\over1{,}920{,}000{,}000}D-o(D)
=(4.11979166667\times10^{-7})D-o(D).
}
\tag{PP.17}

This improves the previous uniform `s_0/8` support-normal-form density.

After partner-degree thinning, the endpoint-disjoint coefficient is

\[
\boxed{
{s_0\over24\,3^{1/8}}
\approx8.97790708869\times10^{-8}.
}
\tag{PP.18}

If the selected mechanism is strong-TB1, every retained bar keeps the fixed order-`D` first-derivative margin. If it is exact-antipodal, every retained birth interface keeps its second force-pair bar and exact positive two-ray reaction certificate.

---

## 7. Stronger no-fixed-margin-TB1 application

On the parameterized deep exact-force branch, the linear nested rail already has one fixed birth side and size

\[
>\lambda D-o(D),
\qquad
\lambda=c_0-6\rho-\eta-2\delta.
\tag{PP.19}

The initial birth-side factor `1/2` is therefore unnecessary. Partition only by the three hubward physical pieces. One obtains either a host-to-host nested rail or a far-terminal nested rail of size

\[
\boxed{>{\lambda\over3}D-o(D).}
\tag{PP.20}

For the ninety-percent choice `lambda=0.9c_0`, this is

\[
\boxed{1.695\times10^{-6}D-o(D).}
\tag{PP.21}

The endpoint-disjoint coefficient is

\[
\boxed{
{\lambda\over12\,3^{1/8}}
\approx3.69376748792\times10^{-7}.
}
\tag{PP.22}

As the fixed parameter losses tend to zero, the linear physical-piece density approaches

\[
{c_0\over3}=1.88333333333\times10^{-6}.
\tag{PP.23}

---

## 8. Frontier effect

The remaining global topology is narrower than the earlier host/death formulation suggested.

A positive-density final blocker population cannot require an arbitrary post-hub death interface as a separate case. After a fixed birth-side extraction, the physical hubward endpoint set itself yields only

```text
fixed host -> fixed hubward host
    with monotone support ends
    (staircase or nested),

or

fixed host -> far terminal boundary piece T_m
    with every support end equal to m.
```

The next non-host target is therefore specifically the far terminal piece, not an arbitrary interior separator. The host branches should be attacked with two-arc endpoint order, near-central diameter geometry, and the common inverse-`D` train grid. The terminal branch should be attacked with terminal-region/source-boundary complexity and the exact resultant/terminal ledgers available at the end of the laminar path.

No C-II or Level-I closure is claimed.

**Disposition:** replaced the `1/8` generic support trichotomy by a `1/6` physical-piece normal form and localized the genuinely non-host plateau to the far terminal boundary piece. C-II and Level I remain open.
